window.COACH = {"page": "hardware/bioreactor", "title": "Hardware · Bioreactor", "prizeName": "Best Hardware", "summary": {"verdict": "On claim discipline this page is ahead of every Best Hardware winner read for this coach. The Purpose section names the design that lost and why hardware answers it, the Results strip labels its own headline claims Partial and Untested with the reason in the same line, 7.1 separates Demonstrated from Not established and gives each gap the experiment that would close it, and 5.1 retracts a wrong specification in public, which nothing in either evidence bank does. Two things hold it back. It is 9,700 words against a winning range of 2,100 to 5,200, and the length is made of repetition: the loop is walked three times, the same five numbers appear in four places, the no-sampling claim is made eight times, and the calibration finding is told three times in one track. And the reproduction package does not survive a walk-through. The materials list cannot say what its own prices mean, does not contain the Luer valves, filters or transducers the assembly guide calls for, and is missing the tubing, cuvette, stirrer, droplet sensor, LED strip and photometer entirely; the guide stops after step eight; there is no sterilisation step, no file location and no licence; and nobody outside the team has touched the machine. Nine numbers also disagree with our own Digital Twin and photometer pages, and two of them, the cuvette path length and the longest run with the membrane fitted, change what the page claims.", "strengths": ["The need argument. \"A release problem became a containment problem, and containment is something hardware can solve.\" The rejected design is named, its failure mode is given as loss of control over expression, dose and spread, and the instrument follows from it. The research found no winner page, undergraduate or over, that derives a device from a rejected design this cleanly.", "Results graded against themselves. Measured, Partial and Untested sit in the strip a judge reads first, and the labels are used against your own claims: breach detection is \"Untested ... pressure transducers and a droplet sensor are fitted; no breach or alarm test is on record\". No precedent in either bank puts a confidence label on a headline claim.", "Limitations with the experiment attached. 7.1's \"Not established\" list and 7.2's Needs lines name eight gaps and what would close each one, down to \"Containment with an n. Two platings, with no replicate count stated for either, and no incubation conditions for the first.\" Neither bioreactor precedent has a limitations section at all.", "Arithmetic a reader can redo. Cycle 5 resolves an impossible rejection rate by showing 42.9% and 14.3% are 6 and 2 of a burst of 14, then writes out OD600 = -log[S / (K.R)] and shows one K near 9.16 reproducing all three dashboard snapshots. We checked it and it closes. Nothing in either bank publishes a reproducible calculation.", "A retraction and a caveat in the same section. 5.1 withdraws the 50 kDa cut-off and the vesicle claim against the part actually bought, and 2.5 states what the 0.2 um pore does not stop. Saying what your own barrier passes is the move that makes a containment argument believable."], "priorities": ["Settle the numbers with the Digital Twin team before anything is frozen. Four of them change claims: the cuvette path length (0.2 mm here and on the photometer page, 2 mm three times on the Twin page, whose factor-of-5.0 arithmetic supports 2 mm), the long run (past 400 hours with 2,132 points over 336 here, 434 hours with 2,671 valid rows there), its strain (WB800 here, 168 there, where that page says every reactor run before October used 168), and the longest run with the membrane fitted (53 h here, against a 102 h run on the full-length hollow fibre module there, inside which your 4 September dashboard snapshot sits).", "Cut a third of the page, starting with the duplications. The loop is walked in the hero cards, in the arrow line in 2.5 and again in eight stages; 300 mL, 94 mL/min, 0.2 um and 200 rpm appear in four places; the in-line claim is repeated eight times; and cycle 6's finding is told in its Test, its Learn and its track box. Your hardware editor's rule is that if the prose and the box beneath it say the same thing, keep one. A judge will not read 9,700 words, which makes the Summary and Results strips the most valuable paragraphs on the page.", "Make the materials list the list for this machine. Re-enter the sheet with quantity, unit cost, line total and a dated exchange rate, add the parts 6.2 and Table 4 require, and reconcile the counts: twelve Luer valves against five, four filters against one, four transducers against three, three reservoirs against two. Until then \"Every line is costed, so the figure above is the build cost\" is not true, and it sits two sentences from the admission that the sheet cannot be interpreted.", "Finish the assembly guide, then say where the files are and under what licence. The guide ends with a vent filter and the machine is not built: no lumen-side plumbing, no culture reservoir, no photometer, no sensors, no LED strip, no priming, no sterilisation and no autoclave plan. This is the only one of our four hardware pages with no downloadable geometry, and none of the four states a licence on its designs.", "Put one person outside the hardware team in front of the rig, and report what happened. Ballot question two is unanswered and 7.3 lists five items, none of which is a person. Having someone build the four membrane segments from 6.2 and write down where they got stuck answers the user-testing question and audits the documentation question in one afternoon."], "ballot": [{"question": "Does the hardware you developed address a need or problem in synthetic biology?", "status": "strong", "where": "Part 2, Purpose, 1.1 to 1.4", "note": "The need is derived from a rejected transgenic design and ends on a requirement hardware can meet, which the research rated the best need argument in this page set, winners included."}, {"question": "Did the team conduct user testing and learn from user feedback?", "status": "missing", "where": "", "note": "No person outside the hardware sub-team is named anywhere on the page as having operated, assembled or reviewed the instrument, and 7.3's deployability list contains no person either."}, {"question": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?", "status": "partial", "where": "3.1 Bacteria Growth, 3.2 cycle 5, 3.4 Bioreactor v1, 5.1 Containment", "note": "Two platings with no colonies, a plateau in two hours, a continuous run past 400 hours and a western blot are real functional evidence. Against that, the integrated run gets one sentence, the pressure and flow track is four empty headings, the flask comparison is uncontrolled, and the alarm path has never been tried against a deliberate fault."}, {"question": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?", "status": "partial", "where": "Part 6, Reproduction, with 4.1 Key Specifications", "note": "Three pump part numbers, a specification table traced to the run that produced each value, and a leak test with a fault tree, against a bill of materials that cannot say what its prices mean and does not match its own assembly guide, a guide that stops after step eight, no CAD or STL, no sterilisation, no software link and no licence."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["Summary and Results", "Part 1 Purpose", "Part 2 Hardware Highlights", "Part 3 Engineering the Bioreactor, four tracks and nine cycles", "Part 4 Final Design (Lab)", "Part 5 Containment and Safety", "Part 6 Reproduction", "Part 7 Limitations and Future Plans", "Part 8 Structural Parts"], "winners": ["Need: what the work requires and what cannot do it now", "Overview of the device, often as a short property list", "Design and build, by prototype or by subsystem", "Demonstration: one run that shows the device does its job", "Reproduction: parts, costs, files, instructions"], "gap": "All five winner slots are present and three of them are stronger than any precedent: the need argument, the design history, and a Limitations part the winners do not have at all. The gap is where the weight sits. Slot four, the demonstration, is the slot four of five winners put last or second to last, and here it is one sentence in 3.4 buried inside a 9,700-word engineering record, while two of the four tracks that record is built on are empty headings. Slot five is positioned correctly and is the weakest content on the page. The practical move is to lift the integrated run and the containment platings into one demonstration block a judge meets early, and to spend the words saved on finishing Part 6."}, "extras": ["The Results row \"Plateau in the reactor Partial OD600 0.8-0.9 in 2 h at 37 degrees C\" gives the flask plateau as about 0.6-0.8 in 3.1. Our Digital Twin page says \"reactor 0.90 by 145 min, flask 0.83 by 278 min\", so the flask figure here reads low. Check both arms against the workbook.", "The blot band is at about 42 kDa here and our Math Model page gives the ACC deaminase subunit as 36.5 kDa with a 144 kDa tetramer. A band running 15% above the subunit mass is normal and worth one clause, because a reader who has both pages open will try to reconcile them.", "The segment list is in centimetres (7.5 cm) and the main assembly in inches (3-inch, 2-inch, 4-inch, 6-inch). Use one unit throughout, because tube length sets the loop volume.", "\"Build\" under growth cycle 2 and under photometer cycle 1 are empty headings. If the build is in the figure, say \"As in Figure 6\" so the heading is not blank.", "Bioreactor v2 and v3 in 3.4 are headings with a cycle label and nothing else. One line each saying what they would test makes them outlined work instead of gaps.", "\"Filter parts 11 / 11 parts\" and \"No parts match that filter\" are both rendered, so the filter control prints a contradiction in its default state.", "The materials list has 11 line items and the table shows 12 component rows. Say in the heading that the reference spectrophotometer is counted separately.", "PES, BPT, TPU and TMP are each used without expansion at least once, and PES is the membrane the whole device depends on. Expand each on first use.", "\"Engineered B. subtilis\" in 3.4 never says which strain or which construct, while 3.1's headers name WB800 and the Twin page names 168 with the v2t1 construct. Name the strain in the test paragraph.", "Figure 21 is referenced in 2.3 and 3.3 and appears in 3.4. If the dashboard is the evidence for the monitoring claim in 2.3, one dashboard image belongs there.", "\"Illustrative: ... The machine is the compact B2 as proposed\" is the only mention of a B2 on the page. Say what B2 is, or name the build the way 3.4 does.", "The Summary says containment \"rests on two platings of shell-side medium\" and 7.1 says the same in its own words two parts later. One of the two can be a pointer."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["model", "software", "protein-design", "protein-design/methodology", "protein-design/sprints", "protein-design/molecular-dynamics", "hardware/bioreactor", "hardware/photometer", "hardware/diopal", "hardware/hydroponics"], "wiki": "655c7af, 2026-10-12", "root": "../../", "notes": [{"id": "br-001", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Either side of the membranePressure sensors ×4", "title": "Four pressure sensors or three", "body": "This panel says four. Section 3.3 and Table 4 name three, feed, retentate and permeate. The materials list in 6.1 has \"Pressure sensor 3\". The assembly draft gives each of the four membrane segments \"1x pressure transducer\", which is four again. Count them on the rig and use one number in all four places.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-002", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Servo valveSwitches to the sampler", "title": "A servo valve appears only here", "body": "This is the only mention of a servo valve on the page. The loop description gives a pinch valve on the return and dual Luer valves for sampling, and the materials list has neither a servo nor a pinch valve. Name the part that is actually fitted.", "after": "Luer valveSwitches to the sampler", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-003", "type": "cut", "anchor": "How it keeps the cells in", "title": "These four numbers appear four times", "body": "300 mL, 94 mL/min, 0.2 um and 200 rpm are given here, again in the Summary strip, again in 2.5's two bullet lists and again in Table 4. Repetition is the thing your hardware editor asked you to cut hardest. Keep them in the Summary strip, where a judge who reads nothing else will see them, and drop this list.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-004", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Cells stay in. Protectant crosses.", "title": "Two cards share one number", "body": "This card and the one above it are both numbered 04, so the walk reads 01, 02, 03, 04, 04, 05, 06, and the route list above it has six stops against seven cards. The two cards also say one thing. Merge them and the numbering fixes itself.", "after": "The culture runs inside hollow fibres. Their walls have 0.2 µm pores, so cells stay in and protectant crosses.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-005", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Illustrative: the liquids and everything that moves or glows", "title": "The figure says what it assumed", "body": "A moving diagram is the easiest place on a hardware page to overstate a device, and this credit line lists exactly which parts of it are drawn rather than measured, down to \"flow direction assumed\". It also says the machine shown is the proposed compact B2 and not the rig the results come from, which is the distinction a judge most needs.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-03"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen gives a full protocol and then says plainly what the experiment was not for. Same move: fence the claim before anyone over-reads it."}, {"id": "br-006", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Nine design‑build‑test‑learn cycles on one perfusion loop, from a prototype that leaked in June to an integrated run in September.", "title": "The first line counts and admits", "body": "One sentence gives the number of cycles, the span of months and the failure the work started from. A judge learns the shape of the record and that you report what broke, before any claim arrives.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-OG-aachen-01"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen's opening lands the need, the device and the reader in three sentences. Yours lands the scope and the honesty in one."}, {"id": "br-007", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The culture reached OD600 0.8–0.9 in two hours at 37 °C", "title": "Four results in one paragraph", "body": "This paragraph carries the growth result, the long photometer run, the western blot and the containment caveat in four clauses joined by commas. A judge who reads only the summary has to unpick them. One sentence per claim, in the order the Results strip uses.", "after": "The culture reached OD600 0.8-0.9 in two hours at 37 °C. The in-line photometer logged a culture for [hours] with no sampling event. ACC deaminase was detected on the shell side by western blot. Containment rests on two platings of shell-side medium, both of which grew no colonies, and neither states a replicate count.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-008", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "the in‑line photometer logged a culture past 400 hours with no sampling event", "title": "Our own pages give this run two lengths", "body": "Our Digital Twin page says \"21 July to 8 August 2026: 434 hours of B. subtilis 168 at 22 °C, 2,671 valid rows, no sample drawn.\" This page and the photometer page say more than 400 hours with 2,132 points over the first 336. If it is one run, one page is wrong; if they are two windows of it, each page has to name its window. The strain differs too, WB800 here and 168 there.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-009", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "the 0.2 µm pore stops whole cells and nothing smaller", "title": "This sentence can be read two ways", "body": "You mean the pore stops whole cells and lets everything smaller through. As written it also reads as \"it stops whole cells, and it stops nothing smaller\", which is the opposite of what 5.1 says. Two words fix it.", "after": "the 0.2 µm pore stops whole cells and passes everything smaller", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-010", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Eight further cycles are outlined and not yet written, the whole pressure‑and‑flow track among them.", "title": "You count what is not written", "body": "Most pages let an empty section speak for itself. This one puts the number of unwritten cycles in the summary and names the track that is missing entirely. A judge who then finds four empty headings in 3.3 has already been told.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06"], "excerpt_note": "TUDarmstadt describes a component at part level and reports it unfinished in the same paragraph. Yours does it for a whole track."}, {"id": "br-011", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "the lumen plate beside one of them grew", "title": "This row stops mid-sentence", "body": "The clause does not say what the lumen plate grew. Section 5.1 says colonies grew from the lumen side, and that is the point: the plate that should grow did, so the shell-side result is not a failed plating. Finish the sentence.", "after": "both clean, beside a lumen-side plate that grew colonies", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-012", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Photometer needs no calibration Measured raw output within 4% of the BioDrop, across two TiO2 runs", "title": "Say which number the four percent is", "body": "Section 3.2 gives three different figures for the same agreement: a median paired difference of 3.8% in test 2, a test 1 fit of y = 1.10x - 0.04, and an RMSE of 0.011 OD600. \"Within 4%\" is the first of those and it comes from one run, not two. Name the statistic and the run so a judge can find it.", "after": "Photometer needs no calibration Measured raw output within a median 3.8% of the BioDrop in test 2, and a second TiO2 run agrees independently", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-013", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Breach detection Untested pressure transducers and a droplet sensor are fitted; no breach or alarm test is on record", "title": "A headline row that says untested", "body": "This row sits in the strip a judge reads first and it labels one of your own safety claims untested, with the reason in the same line. No page in the precedent set puts a confidence label on a headline claim.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06"], "excerpt_note": "TUDarmstadt reports an unfinished component in the same breath as its specification. You do it where it costs most, in the summary."}, {"id": "br-014", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A release problem became a containment problem, and containment is something hardware can solve.", "title": "The best sentence on the page", "body": "The need argument names the design that lost, says what made it fail, and then says why hardware is the answer. Nothing in the five Best Hardware winners read for this coach derives an instrument from a rejected design this cleanly. Do not touch this paragraph.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-01"], "excerpt_note": "TUDarmstadt builds its need in four moves and ends on what a device would have to do. Yours gets there in one sentence."}, {"id": "br-015", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Bacillus subtilis WB800 is well characterized, industrially proven, and native to the rhizosphere.", "title": "Strain biology belongs to the wet lab", "body": "Your editor cut the secretion paragraph off this page for this reason. The only part of this a hardware judge needs is the requirement the machine has to meet: the protectant is secreted, so separation is a size problem. Keep that and send the rest to the wet lab.", "after": "B. subtilis WB800 secretes the protectant into the medium, so the product is already outside the cell and separation is a question of size.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-04"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell lets the organism on a hardware page only as a requirement: what it needs, next to what the part delivers."}, {"id": "br-016", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "the longest run with the membrane module fitted is about 53 hours (7.2)", "title": "Our Twin page has a longer membrane run", "body": "The Digital Twin page lists run 4, 1 to 6 September, as \"168 with constitutive ACC deaminase secretion, in the incubator, full-length hollow fibre module\" and \"102 h from 0.84 to 3.77\". Figure 21 here is dated 4 September and captioned \"53 h into a recording\", which falls inside that window. If they are the same run, your longest membrane run is about 102 hours and this sentence halves your own best result.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-017", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Many peptides degrade in storage, often require a cold chain, and lose potency before use.", "title": "Two paragraphs that argue the market", "body": "Section 1.3 argues shelf life, cold chain and freight cost, none of it hardware and none of it sourced. One sentence carries the consequence a hardware judge needs, which is that the machine has to make the protectant where it is used. The rest belongs on the project description or entrepreneurship page.", "after": "Many peptides degrade in storage and need a cold chain, so the protectant has to be made where it is used. That is the requirement this reactor is built against.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-018", "type": "praise", "anchor": "RELEAF runs predict → produce → protect.", "title": "The device placed in one line", "body": "Three words and the reader knows which stage of the project this machine is and what hands it its input. The strip underneath does the same for the three instruments, upstream, this device, embedded. A judge arriving from any other page is oriented in two sentences.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2023-OG-evry-04"], "excerpt_note": "Evry justifies a second device in one sentence from the first device's need. Same economy."}, {"id": "br-019", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The AIoT stage predicts plant stress and issues a light signal", "title": "AIoT and LPA are never spelled out", "body": "AIoT appears twice on this page and nowhere else in our page set, and it is never expanded. LPA in the next clause is not expanded either, and it is the name of one of our own instruments. Our judges read many pages a day and are not in our field.", "after": "The stress-prediction stage (AIoT, artificial intelligence of things) predicts plant stress and issues a light signal; this bioreactor is the produce stage that receives it. The light-programmable array (LPA) is the instrument built to establish which light conditions drive protectant expression", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-020", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "A machine intended to run unattended on a farm must be able to report its own condition.", "title": "Section 2.4 is missing", "body": "The next heading is 2.5, so either 2.4 was deleted or its number was skipped. Nothing on the page cross-references 2.4, so no pointer is broken, but a judge counting sections will notice. Renumber, or drop the numbers the way your editor's edit does.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-021", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Everything in the record below refers to one of two fluid paths. They meet only at the membrane wall.", "title": "Two paths named before the detail", "body": "This gives the reader the one abstraction the rest of the page depends on, in two short sentences, before a single component appears. Everything after it can then be placed by saying which side it is on.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-01"], "excerpt_note": "Humboldt's opening doubles as a specification sheet, so a reader can decide in one paragraph whether the instrument suits them. Yours does the same job for the plumbing."}, {"id": "br-022", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Medium reservoir → peristaltic pump → in‑line photometer → fiber lumens → back to the reservoir.", "title": "The loop is walked three times", "body": "This arrow line gives the route in eleven words. The hero cards above walk it in seven panels, and \"The system, stage by stage\" below walks it again in eight. Keep this line and keep the stages that add something the line does not, which are the harvest, the sensing and the delivery. Stages 1 to 4 repeat this sentence.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-023", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The pump acts on the outside of the tubing only, so the fluid path is not breached at the pump head.", "title": "A containment claim with its mechanism", "body": "One clause of mechanism and one of consequence. A reader who has never seen a peristaltic pump now knows why that pump was chosen and what it buys, and the claim is checkable against the part.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-OG-aachen-02"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen writes its device for a stranger who might build it. This sentence is in that register."}, {"id": "br-024", "type": "cut", "anchor": "shell‑side medium plated in cycle 1 produced no colonies (5.1). The stream was cell‑free in both platings on record.", "title": "Two sentences, one fact", "body": "These say the same thing twice, once about cycle 1 and once about both platings. Keep the second, which carries more and still points at 5.1.", "after": "Anything in that stream crossed the fiber wall, whose 0.2 µm pore is smaller than a B. subtilis cell; the stream was cell-free in both platings on record (5.1).", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-025", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Shell‑side fluid passes a pH regulator on its way out to the delivery stages.", "title": "The pH regulator is never specified", "body": "This stage is one sentence and the regulator appears nowhere else on the page: no part, no setpoint, no reagent, no line in the materials list. The hero figure says \"pH controlKOH pump\". If KOH is the reagent, say so here and put the pump in 6.1.", "after": "Shell-side fluid passes a pH regulator on its way out to the delivery stages. It holds the product stream at [setpoint] by dosing [reagent] with the [part] pump.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-026", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Culture returns down the lumens and through a pinch valve to the reservoir.", "title": "Mark which stages are fitted", "body": "The walk is headed \"as designed\", and three of its parts are not in the build: this pinch valve is listed in Table 4 as outlined for integration cycle 2, and the shell-side optical sensor and pH regulator have no line in the materials list. A reader cannot tell which stages exist. One word per stage, fitted or designed, settles it.", "after": "Culture returns down the lumens to the reservoir. A pinch valve on that return is outlined for integration cycle 2 and is not fitted yet (3.4).", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-027", "type": "praise", "anchor": "What crosses the membrane wall and goes to the field is the protectant together with anything else below the 0.2 µm pore", "title": "You name what the barrier passes", "body": "Naming \"medium components, vesicles, debris\" is what makes the containment argument honest, and no precedent in the bank says what its own barrier fails to stop. The last clause, that the Luer valves are the one place procedure still matters, tells a reader exactly where the human risk sits.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-03"], "excerpt_note": "Humboldt turns a plot into a working range and says what would extend it. You turn a pore rating into a stated limit."}, {"id": "br-028", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Four tracks, each with its own design–build–test–learn cycles", "title": "The record's shape given up front", "body": "Four tracks named in one sentence, in a physical order, with the convention for unwritten cycles stated before the reader meets one. For a record of nine cycles this paragraph is what keeps a judge from getting lost.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-029", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Culture B. subtilis WB800 at room temperature (22 °C)", "title": "This run may not be WB800", "body": "Our Digital Twin page says \"Every reactor run before October used B. subtilis 168\" and lists both June runs as 168. Both headers in 3.1 say WB800. The two pages cannot both be right about June. Check the lab record and write one strain on both pages, because the strain is part of every growth number here.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-030", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Final OD600 readings were 0.304 in the tube and 0.48 in the medium reservoir", "title": "Your own caption ends this run lower", "body": "Figure 4's caption says the bioreactor culture went \"from about 0.17 to 0.3-0.37 by the end of the run\", and our Digital Twin page says \"reactor 0.17 to 0.37\". The 0.48 reservoir reading sits above all of those. Either it is a different time point, and the sentence should say which, or it is a typo.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-031", "type": "praise", "anchor": "To check whether any viable cells had crossed the membrane, 2 mL of medium was drawn from the shell side and plated on LB agar.", "title": "The volume plated is on the page", "body": "The question, the volume, the medium and the result in one sentence, and the next clause fences the claim to a single plating. That is the whole containment test written so another team could repeat it.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-03"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen gives the protocol and then names what the experiment was not for. Same discipline, fewer words."}, {"id": "br-032", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Single plating; replicate count and incubation time and temperature were not recorded (5.1).", "title": "A caption that states its own n", "body": "Only one page in either evidence bank puts measurement conditions in its captions, and none of them lists the conditions that were not recorded. This clause costs you nothing and it is the reason a judge will believe the rest of your platings.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06"], "excerpt_note": "TUDarmstadt is the only precedent whose captions carry measurement conditions. Yours carry the missing ones too."}, {"id": "br-033", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The divergent OD600 values between the tube and the reservoir indicated poor growth and pointed to two hardware limitations for the next cycle.", "title": "Two limitations, then three fixes", "body": "The paragraph promises two hardware limitations and then lists three changes: BPT tubing, a magnetic stirrer and a moved sampling port. Name the faults, then the fix for each, in the same order.", "after": "The divergent OD600 values between the tube and the reservoir pointed to two faults: no active mixing in the reservoir, and a seal that leaked at the pump head. We planned a magnetic stirrer for the first, on the model of a laboratory hot-plate stirrer, and biocompatible BPT tubing for the second. We also moved the sampling port to the reservoir outlet, for a cleaner reading and less contamination risk.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-034", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "the bioreactor reached its plateau (OD600 ~0.8–0.9) after 2 hours, the flask (OD600 ~0.6–0.8) after 4.5 hours", "title": "Say what would have counted as success", "body": "The run is reported without a criterion set beforehand, so a reader cannot tell whether plateau in two hours was the target or a surprise. Aachen's chemostat states the pass condition before the result. One clause before these numbers does the same work here.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-01"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen fixes the success criterion first: hold each dilution rate for three reactor volumes and see whether OD stays constant within sampling error.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-035", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Both arms used the same medium and temperature, but that is not a controlled comparison.", "title": "You take your own result apart", "body": "The comparison is set up and then disarmed in the same section: the three unrecorded flask variables are named, the three-variables-at-once problem is stated, and the reader is told how far to trust it. \"A promising difference, not a measured one\" is the sentence a judge will quote back.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-03"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen fences off a sensor test so nobody reads it as a bioprocess result. You fence off your own headline growth claim."}, {"id": "br-036", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "passing the culture through a flow cuvette with a 0.2 mm path", "title": "Our Twin page says ten times this", "body": "This page and the photometer page say a 0.2 mm path. The Digital Twin page says a 2 mm flow cuvette three times, and its own arithmetic supports 2 mm: \"a path length factor of 5.0, so a 2 mm cell is reported on a 1 cm equivalent scale\", and 10 mm over 2 mm is 5. At 0.2 mm that factor would be 50. Your cycle 5 notes record the dashboard reporting five times the computed value. Path length scales OD directly, so measure the cuvette and write one figure on all three pages.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-037", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Fixing everything else made the lens position the single variable.", "title": "One variable, said in one line", "body": "The method is justified by what it isolates, and the next sentences say what it bought: real distances for the CAD instead of guesses. The closing clause, \"We did not have to guess at them\", is the reason the three spacings that follow can be trusted.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen writes assembly for someone holding the parts. This is the same voice applied to a measurement."}, {"id": "br-038", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "LED tip to lens", "title": "Three spacings and no tolerance", "body": "These three distances are the fixed geometry for every build after cycle 1 and they are given to the nearest half centimetre with no tolerance. V1 then failed because loose mounts let the geometry drift, so you already know the alignment is sensitive. One tolerance per spacing is what a team rebuilding this cannot work out for itself.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen's construction steps give what to glue where and what changes between builds. Numbers a builder can hold to.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-039", "type": "praise", "anchor": "It parks in the light and stays there until flow happens to push it out, so a single bubble can corrupt a long stretch of readings.", "title": "A fault explained by its mechanism", "body": "The reader learns why a horizontal light path fails, not only that it did, and the next build follows from this sentence without having to be announced. A judge can check the reasoning without the data.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-UG-ubc-04"], "excerpt_note": "UBC introduces a feature by the prototype that forced it. Your V2 is forced by this paragraph."}, {"id": "br-040", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Adjustability helped during alignment and hurt everywhere else.", "title": "A design rule from one build", "body": "Nine words turn a build annoyance into a rule another team can use. This is the move the 2024 undergraduate winner was rewarded for, and you do it in one line.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-UG-ubc-02"], "excerpt_note": "UBC turns a printer tolerance problem into a transferable design rule. Same shape, shorter."}, {"id": "br-041", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "[TABLE] Table 1 V2 readings", "title": "Table 1 is on a different scale", "body": "The OD column here reads 62.5 au for 2 mL of titanium dioxide. Table 2, two cycles later, reads 0.5785 au for 2 mL in the same cell. The two tables cannot both be OD600 in the same units. Say what au is and how it is computed, or correct the figure.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-042", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The reference channel held at 92.5 lux across both readings.", "title": "The control read before the claim", "body": "You check that the source was stable before attributing the drop to the sample, and then say exactly which inference that licenses. Two sentences, and the table above becomes evidence instead of numbers.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-02"], "excerpt_note": "TU Darmstadt states how two instruments were read against each other before reporting agreement. Same order of operations."}, {"id": "br-043", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The optics were not the main problem. Our calibration procedure was.", "title": "A diagnosis that corrects yourselves", "body": "Two short sentences, then the mechanism: you blanked on growth medium, the BioDrop on distilled water, so a gap you had been blaming on your own optics was procedural. The protocol that came out of it is stated in the next line and used from then on.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-04"], "excerpt_note": "TU Darmstadt reports its instrument reading low and then explains the mechanism in its own calibration. Yours finds the error in the procedure and says so."}, {"id": "br-044", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "the roughly 20% disagreement we had been attributing to our optics was largely a procedural artifact", "title": "The twenty percent is not on the page", "body": "This is the only mention of a 20% disagreement, and no table in cycles 1 to 3 shows it. A judge cannot check the finding your whole calibration protocol came out of. Give the two readings it was computed from, or say where they are recorded.", "after": "the roughly 20% disagreement we had been attributing to our optics, [our reading] against the BioDrop's [reading], was largely a procedural artifact", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-045", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Photometer · Cycle 4", "title": "V3 is missing from the version list", "body": "The builds run V1, V2, an unnumbered \"Angled stand\", then V4. There is no V3 anywhere on the page. If the angled stand is V3, call it V3. If it is not, say what V3 was, because a reader meeting V4 after V2 assumes a build was dropped from the record.", "after": "Photometer · Cycle 4 · V3", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-046", "type": "praise", "anchor": "We put the tilt in a wedge under the body instead of the optics, which keeps the cycle 1 beam geometry untouched", "title": "A change that protects what works", "body": "The design decision is justified by what it does not disturb, and the second half gives a reader the knob to turn: reprint the wedge at a different angle without touching the alignment. That is a reproducible design, not only a finished one.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2023-OG-evry-05"], "excerpt_note": "Evry names the rejected alternative and the reason it lost. You name the alternative placement and what it would have cost."}, {"id": "br-047", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Doubling the titanium dioxide only moved the reading by a factor of 1.56, which we cannot yet explain from this data alone.", "title": "Name the two explanations you have", "body": "Saying you cannot explain it is right. A judge would rather see the candidates, and two sit in your own record: the open light path this same paragraph blames for the reference drift, and the compression at high turbidity the photometer page describes. Name both and leave them unresolved.", "after": "Doubling the titanium dioxide only moved the reading by a factor of 1.56. Two candidates sit in this record, stray room light reaching the sample sensor and compression of the reading at high turbidity, and this run cannot separate them.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-05"], "excerpt_note": "TU Darmstadt gives the threshold above which a reading is usable and draws the general rule. A named limit beats a general caution."}, {"id": "br-048", "type": "praise", "anchor": "It moved 6.4% between the blank and the samples, and the reference beam is split off before the sample, so nothing in the cuvette should be able to change it.", "title": "The number, then why it is impossible", "body": "You compute the drift from your own table, say why the geometry forbids it, list the two candidates and then draw the consequence: a reference channel we cannot trust is a reference channel we cannot correct with. We checked the arithmetic and 18.33 over 17.23 is 6.4%.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-05"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen reports two calibration coefficients and then judges the gap between them. Same habit of grading your own number."}, {"id": "br-049", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Titanium dioxide tells us the optics work, but it does not move, does not grow, and does not produce bubbles.", "title": "You fence off what a standard proves", "body": "Three clauses say what the stable scatterer cannot test, and the next sentence sets the bar the instrument still has to clear. A judge reading a calibration section is looking for exactly this sentence and usually does not find it.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-03"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen says plainly what its side experiment was not for. Yours says what its standard was not for."}, {"id": "br-050", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A photometer that responds to the lights being switched on is not measuring the sample.", "title": "The requirement stated as a failure", "body": "The snap-in covers are justified by naming the failure they prevent, in one sentence a non-specialist understands immediately. Every one of the three V4 changes is written this way, which is why that bullet list reads faster than the prose around it.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-OG-aachen-02"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen addresses a stranger who might build the device. This bullet does the same for a design choice."}, {"id": "br-051", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "The instrument runs on an Arduino UNO Q. Both lux sensors report over the same bus.", "title": "Name the software and who wrote it", "body": "The dashboard is the only way to read this instrument and the page never names the program, its version, its author or where it lives. Our Digital Twin page does: \"the Arduino App Lab application our instructor Chars wrote for the Uno Q board on BR-01\", interface v4.13. Humboldt's answer to this ballot question was one tagged release holding assembly instructions, parts list and software together.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-06"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell names the deliverable another team needs and itemises what is in it. One sentence, and the reader knows what to download.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-052", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The run passed 400 hours.", "title": "Say where the other sixty hours are", "body": "The run passed 400 hours and Figure 16 traces the first 336. Nothing says what the remaining hours show or why the trace stops, and our Digital Twin page puts the total at 434. One sentence closes it.", "after": "The run passed 400 hours. Figure 16 traces the first 336 h, the window captured from the live dashboard; the remaining hours [what they show].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-053", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The run covers lag through decline in one unbroken series, which is the thing a manual sampling routine cannot produce.", "title": "The Twin page calls the decline a fault", "body": "Our Digital Twin page says of this run \"the OD channel failed\" and marks a sensor alarm at 216.3 h, which falls inside the 336 h traced here. If the late decline is the instrument and not the culture, this series cannot be read as lag through decline. Settle which hours are trustworthy and put the answer in Figure 16's caption.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-054", "type": "praise", "anchor": "each reading is a burst of 14, and 42.9% and 14.3% are exactly 6 and 2 of 14", "title": "An inconsistency chased to its cause", "body": "A rejection rate that looked impossible is resolved by arithmetic a reader can redo, and you say that the rule had to be written down before filtered data could be published. That is a judge's own objection answered before they raise it.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-05"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen reports two independently measured coefficients and then judges the gap. Yours resolves the gap and names the rule it needed."}, {"id": "br-055", "type": "praise", "anchor": "with the factor, all three snapshots above reproduce with one K ≈ 9.16", "title": "A formula a reader can check", "body": "The calculation is written out and then shown to reproduce every row of the table above it. We redid it: 171.67 over 9.16 times 26.67 gives 0.153, and five times that is 0.765 against the 0.7661 on the dashboard. Nothing in either evidence bank publishes a reproducible calculation like this.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-02"], "excerpt_note": "TU Darmstadt gives the correlation coefficient and the transform. You give the formula and show it closing on your own data."}, {"id": "br-056", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The dashboard expects a sample‑to‑reference ratio near 10:1 and we measured 5.3× to 6.4×, so either the split ratio or the expectation is wrong.", "title": "Say which splitter is fitted", "body": "Our photometer page says a 90:10 splitter and our Digital Twin page says the splitter ran at 9:1. Both come to the 10:1 expectation, and your measurement is about half of it. Name the part and its rated split, so a reader can tell whether the part or the expectation is at fault.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-057", "type": "praise", "anchor": "In Test 2 the 5 mg BioDrop reading breaks monotonicity", "title": "Exclusions justified and still drawn", "body": "Two points are excluded, each on a stated objective ground, and both stay on the plot as open markers. The clause that puts the error in the reference measurement because the paired RELEAF reading is on trend is the part a judge will trust.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-05"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen judges the gap between its own two coefficients instead of picking the better one. Same refusal to quietly tidy data."}, {"id": "br-058", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "plotted as RELEAF against BioDrop, Test 1 has a slope near 1 and an intercept near 0", "title": "A slope of 1.10 is ten percent", "body": "The fit printed under this sentence is y = 1.10x - 0.04. A slope of 1.10 means the two instruments differ by about 10% at the top of the range, which is not \"near 1\" for a reader who is about to trust a reading. Give the number and what it costs.", "after": "plotted as RELEAF against BioDrop, Test 1 gives a slope of 1.10 and an intercept of -0.04, so our reading runs about 10% above the BioDrop at the top of the range", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-059", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "RELEAF photometer y = 0.03x + 0.0448 · R² 0.9999 · n 18BioDrop y = 0.0318x + 0.00163 · R² 0.9998 · n 19", "title": "The excluded point comes off the wrong fit", "body": "Test 2's exclusion is a BioDrop reading, so the BioDrop fit should be the one that loses a point. Here the BioDrop has n 19 and RELEAF n 18. The direct-agreement fit below also has n 19, and a paired plot cannot hold more points than its shorter series. Check all three counts against the workbook.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-060", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Test 1, 2–40 mg: 0.030 against 0.031 OD600", "title": "Test 1 runs to 41 mg, not 40", "body": "The fit label and the figure caption above both say 2 to 41 mg, with n 39. This line says 2 to 40 mg. One of the two is a typo and a reader checking the workbook stops here.", "after": "Test 1, 2-41 mg: 0.030 against 0.031 OD600", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-061", "type": "praise", "anchor": "the BioDrop is itself the slightly less linear of the two instruments against the ground‑truth standard", "title": "You argue against your own plan", "body": "The cycle was built to produce a calibration transform and it concludes the transform would make the readings worse, with the reason given as a comparison against the mass standard instead of against the reference instrument. Then you say every reading in the record is raw, which tells a judge no number on the page has been quietly adjusted.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-03"], "excerpt_note": "TU Darmstadt keeps validation separate from calibration and runs fresh samples through the fit. You go one step further and reject the fit."}, {"id": "br-062", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Two properties emerged from this cycle.", "title": "This paragraph repeats the Learn above it", "body": "The first property restates agreement already given twice in this cycle. The second, that the instrument reads in line without withdrawing a sample, is stated in 2.3, in stage 2 of 2.5, in the Results strip and again in 7.1. Keep the one clause none of them has.", "after": "A cuvette spectrophotometer cannot monitor a live bioreactor; it measures grab samples one at a time. Cycle 5 showed this instrument reading the same culture continuously, which is what closed-loop monitoring needs.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-063", "type": "cut", "anchor": "The calibration transform this track was built to produce turned out to be unnecessary.", "title": "The third telling of one finding", "body": "This box repeats the Learn paragraph of cycle 6, which repeats the Test paragraph above it. A track summary earns its place by saying something the cycles did not, for example which build the photometer in the final design is.", "after": "The calibration transform this track was built to produce turned out to be unnecessary, so no transform is applied to any reading here. The photometer in the final design is [version].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-064", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Four cycles are outlined here and none has been run.", "title": "The dashboard already has the numbers", "body": "Four empty cycles sit above a figure that logs feed, retentate and permeate pressure, and Table 4 reports all three from 4 September. Report that one reading here, with the flow it was taken at, and the track stops being empty. Aachen shows the shape for a single control observation: what was set, how long it held, what changed and what it did to the measurement.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-02"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen reports a pump imbalance found mid-run in sequence, with the correction and its measured cost in OD. One paragraph, and the reader trusts the rig.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-065", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Transmembrane pressure is the quantity they would measure, and it is the record's highest open parameter (7.2).", "title": "Say what a healthy TMP looks like", "body": "This section names TMP as its subject and the record's highest open parameter without a number anywhere: no expected range, no alarm threshold, no value from the log that is already running. Section 5.2 makes an abnormally low TMP a safety signal, so the threshold is a safety number too.", "after": "Transmembrane pressure is the quantity they would measure, and it is the record's highest open parameter (7.2). We expect [range] bar on a clean module at 94 mL/min, and 5.2 treats anything below [threshold] as a possible breach.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-066", "type": "praise", "anchor": "ACC deaminase is placed under a constitutive promoter so the strain expresses continuously, which removes light control as a variable.", "title": "One variable removed, and said so", "body": "The biology on this page is doing the one job it should do on a hardware page, which is to make the test about the loop. Saying so in the sentence that introduces the cycle stops a judge reading this as an optogenetics result.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-04"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell lets the organism in only as a requirement and a constraint. Yours lets it in as a variable deliberately held still."}, {"id": "br-067", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Engineered B. subtilis was run in the system and ACC deaminase was secreted across the membrane (Figure 22).", "title": "The one integrated run gets one line", "body": "This is the demonstration the proof-of-concept question turns on, and it is a single sentence whose caption carries more than it does. Give the run its conditions here: strain, medium, volume, temperature, duration and flow. TUDarmstadt crosses into biology exactly once per device and that one paragraph has all of them.", "after": "Engineered B. subtilis [strain] was run in the loop in [medium] at [temperature] for [duration] at 94 mL/min, and ACC deaminase was detected in shell-side medium sampled from 14 to 44 h (Figure 22).", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-07"], "excerpt_note": "TUDarmstadt's culture test gives volume, inoculum, strain, time and the one observation it supports. One paragraph, then back to hardware.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-068", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "ACC deaminase was successfully secreted to the shell side, but expression is weak.", "title": "Weak against what", "body": "\"Weak\" has no reference here. A blot band is weak relative to a loading control, to the lumen-side lanes or to a standard. Say which comparison you mean and what the next measurement would be. 7.2 already names it, so the pointer is written.", "after": "ACC deaminase reached the shell side. The band is faint against the lumen-side lanes on the same blot, so the amount crossing is small and has not been quantified (7.2).", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-069", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "the ACC deaminase band is marked at ~42 kDa. At this size a shell‑side band is faint at best.", "title": "Give the protein a size the pore can judge", "body": "The release claim in 2.5 rests on the protectant being small enough to pass a 0.2 um pore, and a kDa figure does not settle that. Our Math Model page has the number that does: the active enzyme is a homotetramer of about 144 kDa with a 9.4 nm hydrodynamic size, against a 200 nm pore. Put that comparison beside the blot and \"passes the protectant freely\" stops being an assertion.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-04"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell puts what the part delivers next to what the organism needs. Same move: the pore next to the molecule.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-070", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The values below are the ones stated elsewhere in this record and in the draft assembly guide (6.2); where the final design's value is not yet recorded, the row says so.", "title": "Specifications traced to their run", "body": "Both bioreactor precedents state their specifications as settled facts without saying which run established them. This sentence, and the cycle pointer in each row, make the table an audit of the record instead of a claim about the machine. The rows that disagree with themselves are visible because of it.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-01"], "excerpt_note": "Humboldt's opening gives configurations, volumes and ranges as a specification sheet. Yours adds the provenance that theirs leaves out."}, {"id": "br-071", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "about 371 mL/min on the 4 September integration‑v1 dashboard (Figure 21)", "title": "Two flow rates, four times apart", "body": "Recording both without choosing is honest and it leaves a reader unable to use either. 94 mL/min appears five times elsewhere on the page as the flow rate. Say which is the design setting and what the other one was: a different pump, a different tube bore, or a dashboard reading in different units.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-072", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The assembly guide's parts list has two 1 L reservoirs and a 100 mL one.", "title": "Three reservoir sets in one record", "body": "This row holds two of them, 300 mL in a 500 mL vessel and the guide's two 1 L plus a 100 mL. The materials list in 6.1 has a third, \"Reservoir 2\" with no volumes. A reader buying glassware cannot tell which set to buy or what each vessel is for.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-073", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Feed, retentate and permeate read about 0.20 bar each on the 4 September dashboard", "title": "Equal pressures mean zero TMP", "body": "If all three read 0.20 bar, the transmembrane pressure computed from them is about zero, and 3.3 calls TMP the record's highest open parameter. Either the figures are rounded past the difference that matters, or the transducers were reading the same point. Give the three values to the precision the dashboard shows, and the TMP that follows.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-074", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "3 m green‑light LED strip, switched by the stress prediction to induce expression (1.4).", "title": "The induction light is never measured", "body": "This strip is the part that turns the machine on, and the page gives its length and nothing else: no intensity at the vessel wall, no uniformity, no delivered dose, no part number. Humboldt measured light at 24 positions at three settings and published the distribution inside the vessel. One reading at the vessel wall, at the working setting, would be enough here.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-01"], "excerpt_note": "Humboldt's opening states the wavelength range the instrument delivers, because that is what a phototroph user has to check.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-075", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "green at about 526 nm to switch expression on and red at about 670 nm to switch it off (CcaS–CcaR)", "title": "Green is 535 nm on our other pages", "body": "Our DiOPAL page says \"Around 535 nm, which induces protectant expression\" and our Math Model page says \"Green light at 535 nm activates the sensor kinase CcaS\". Red is 670 here and 672 on the Math Model. The brochure draft is the only source for 526 nm. Agree one pair across the wiki and cite it once.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-076", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Making sure synthetic organisms do not enter the wild is a responsibility for everyone working with genetically modified organisms.", "title": "Two sentences that say nothing yet", "body": "This opening states that biosafety matters and that it matters to us. The section's real claim is much stronger and arrives one heading later: containment here is physical, at a pore smaller than the cell. Start there.", "after": "Containment in this reactor is physical. The only thing holding B. subtilis inside the machine is a membrane whose pores are smaller than the cells.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-077", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "its pore size, 0.2 µm, is smaller than the cell diameter of B. subtilis, 0.25 to 1.0 µm", "title": "The safety claim needs a citation", "body": "All of 5.1 rests on this one range and no source is given for it. A judge checking a containment argument will look for one. Say also whether the 0.2 um figure is a nominal rating or an absolute one, because a nominal rating passes some particles above its size and that matters more here than anywhere else on the page.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-078", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Drawn to scale from the values above: the membrane's 0.2 µm pore, and B. subtilis cells at the smallest and largest diameters quoted.", "title": "A caption that says what is not to scale", "body": "The caption names the two values drawn, states the conclusion a reader should take from the drawing, and then says the wall is not to scale so nobody reads a thickness off it. That last clause is the one almost every page forgets.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06"], "excerpt_note": "TUDarmstadt's captions carry measurement conditions. Yours carries the limits of the drawing."}, {"id": "br-079", "type": "praise", "anchor": "An earlier version of this specification claimed a 50 kDa molecular‑weight cut‑off and stated that the membrane retains secreted membrane vesicles.", "title": "A retraction, in public, with the reason", "body": "Nothing in either evidence bank retracts anything. This paragraph names the wrong claim, says why it was wrong for the part actually bought, draws the consequence that containment now rests on cell size alone, and dates the withdrawal. A judge who finds this believes the rest of the page.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-OG-aachen-03"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen reports a second, worse result instead of dropping it, and grades the degradation. Yours reports a wrong claim instead of deleting it."}, {"id": "br-080", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Neither has been tried against a deliberate leak or a deliberate breach, so the alarm path is not yet tested (7.1).", "title": "Both alarms are an afternoon's test", "body": "You name this gap exactly, and it is the cheapest result left on the page. A few drops of water under the droplet sensor, and a loosened Luer fitting with the pressure log running, each with the response written down. You already have the shape in 6.2's own leak test, and TUDarmstadt's reproduction package is a leak test and an air test with a stated result each.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-UG-ubc-01"], "excerpt_note": "UBC sets a target, measures against it, and reports what the measurement turned out to depend on. A pass condition makes a safety test reportable.", "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-081", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Every line is costed, so the figure above is the build cost.", "title": "This cannot be the build cost", "body": "Two sentences later the page says \"The sheet does not say whether a price is per unit or per line\". If the prices are per unit, four pumps at 2,500 come to 10,000 and the parts total is wrong. The list also has no tubing, no flow cuvette, no droplet sensor, no magnetic stirrer, no LED strip, no pinch valve and no photometer, all of which 6.2 and Table 4 require. NT$16,450 is a column total, not the cost of this machine.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-082", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Costs are as entered on the team's component sheet, in New Taiwan dollars.", "title": "Re-enter the sheet with unit costs", "body": "Being honest about an ambiguous sheet is better than hiding it, and the fix is one afternoon. Four columns close it: quantity, unit cost, line total, and the date of the exchange rate. Aachen scopes its estimate in a sentence before the numbers, and Humboldt gives seven subsystem lines to one figure a team can hold against its own budget.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-04", "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-04"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen names what is being totalled before totalling it. Humboldt gives the reason for publishing a price before the price.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-083", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "about US$960 at the sheet's own conversion", "title": "Say which total the dollars convert", "body": "US$960 is about NT$30,450, the sheet total with the reference spectrophotometer, and not the NT$16,450 the previous sentence calls the build cost. As written the dollar figure reads as the build cost. Name the figure it converts and give the rate.", "after": "and its total adds the column, NT$30,450, or about US$960 at the sheet's own rate of [rate] on [date]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-084", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "One segment connects each of the membrane's four ports", "title": "The two parts lists do not add up", "body": "Four segments at two Luer valves each is eight, plus the four in the main assembly list is twelve, against \"Luer valve 5\" in 6.1. Each segment also takes one filter and one pressure transducer, which is four of each, against \"Air filter 1\" and \"Pressure sensor 3\". A reader buying from 6.1 cannot build the segments in 6.2. Make the two lists agree, with quantities.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-085", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Cut the tubing into three sections, A, B and C.", "title": "Say how long each section is", "body": "The segment list gives 7.5 cm of tubing and three sections and never says how to divide it. It also never gives the tubing bore, which is what decides the flow the pump delivers. Two numbers here make the segment buildable.", "after": "Cut the 7.5 cm of tubing into three sections: A at [length], B at [length] and C at [length], all of [bore] inner bore.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-086", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Hold the right port facing up and feed 3–5 drops of water into it with a dropper, then slowly rotate the handle of the center valve and watch for leaks.", "title": "A check written for someone holding it", "body": "The quantity, the orientation, the action and then the fault tree, in two sentences and in the second person. This is the best-written passage in the reproduction section and it is the register the rest of the guide needs.", "excerpts": ["hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen's construction steps tell a reader what to glue where and what changes between builds. Same voice."}, {"id": "br-087", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Connect the other end of the In‑Shell segment to one end of the flow cuvette with a 3‑inch tube.", "title": "Which optical device is this", "body": "This step puts the flow cuvette on the In-Shell segment. Section 2.5 puts the photometer's flow cell in the culture loop, on the lumen side, and gives the shell side a separate \"optical sensor\". The materials list has one flow cuvette and no optical sensor. Say which instrument this step fits and which side it reads, because a shell-side stream has no cells in it to measure.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-088", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Connect a 2‑inch tube from the “Out” of a filter to another hole in the cap of the shell‑side reservoir.", "title": "The guide stops after eight steps", "body": "This is the last step, and the machine is not built: no lumen-side plumbing, no culture reservoir, no photometer, no pH sensor, no droplet sensor, no stirrer placement, no LED strip, no priming and no sterilisation. TUDarmstadt publishes seven numbered steps with a photograph each. Finishing this from material already on the page is the single biggest thing you can do for the reproduction question.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-06"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell names its operating manual and itemises what is in it: assembly, operation, precautions, and the differences between variants.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-089", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "The build, segment by segmentworking draft", "title": "No files and no licence for the build", "body": "This is the only one of our four hardware pages with no downloadable geometry, and none of the four states a licence on its designs. Aachen 2015 carries a licence in every footer and releases its control software under GPL v3; Humboldt puts assembly instructions, parts list and software in one tagged release. Say where the CAD, the print files and the dashboard code live, and under what licence.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-06"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell's one sentence about its manual is the cheapest version of this. Name the artifact and what is in it.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-090", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "B. subtilis WB800; engineered strains in the 400 h run and integration v1", "title": "No sterilisation anywhere in the guide", "body": "Section 6.3 gives the organism, the medium, the inoculum and the temperature, and nothing on the page says how any of it is sterilised or which parts survive an autoclave. The membrane cartridge, the printed photometer body and the pumps cannot go in together. Cornell answers this in one sentence by naming a dedicated autoclave configuration and assembling the rest afterwards.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-UG-cornell-03"], "excerpt_note": "Cornell lists its materials and then justifies them by one consequence: the assembly can be autoclaved. Biology enters a hardware page as a constraint.", "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-091", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "LB, 300 mL working volume", "title": "Name the medium precisely", "body": "LB has several standard recipes and the page never says which, or whether it carries an antibiotic. Our Digital Twin page says the WB800 repeat ran \"under antibiotic selection\". A team reproducing your growth curves needs the recipe and the selection.", "after": "LB [recipe], 300 mL working volume, with [antibiotic] at [concentration] where selection is used", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Is the documentation of the hardware system sufficient to enable reproduction by other teams?"}, {"id": "br-092", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Containment with an n. Two platings, with no replicate count stated for either, and no incubation conditions for the first.", "title": "The gap named as an experiment", "body": "Every line of \"Not established\" names the claim, the evidence under it and what is missing, and this one turns the missing thing into something a reader could go and do. Neither bioreactor precedent has a limitations section at all. This is the strongest section on the page.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-03"], "excerpt_note": "Humboldt converts a validation plot into a working range and says what would extend it. Yours does that for eight claims at once."}, {"id": "br-093", "type": "praise", "anchor": "No aeration path is described for the cycle 2 rig (the later assembly draft adds a vent filter, 6.2), and dissolved oxygen was not measured, so oxygen limitation is not excluded.", "title": "An alternative explanation left open", "body": "You offer a competing cause for your own headline growth result and say what would rule it out. Our Digital Twin page reaches the same place from the other side, that stirring was added \"to give the aerobe more oxygen\". A judge reading both pages finds them agreeing on a weakness, which is rarer than agreeing on a result.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-03"], "excerpt_note": "Aachen refuses to let a sensor test be read as a bioprocess result. You refuse to let a plateau be read as a throughput limit."}, {"id": "br-094", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The longest recording known to have the membrane module fitted is about two days (53 h, integration cycle 1).", "title": "Check this against run four", "body": "The Digital Twin page's run 4 is logged with the \"full-length hollow fibre module\" and runs 102 hours, 2 to 6 September. Figure 21 here is a 4 September snapshot at 53 h, which falls inside that window. If they are the same run, this line and 1.2 both understate your best membrane run by half, and the open parameter in this block is partly answered already.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team demonstrate utility and functionality in their hardware proof of concept?"}, {"id": "br-095", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The 400 h photometer record was drawn from the bioreactor loop, in the doc's words.", "title": "In the doc's words is not a source", "body": "\"The doc\" appears in 4.1, in 6.1 and here, and the page never says what it is or where a reader could see it. If it is an internal working document, cite it by name and date. If the claim is yours, own it, and say whether the membrane module was fitted for that run, because that is the whole question this block is asking.", "after": "The 400 h photometer record was drawn from the bioreactor loop, recorded in [document, date]. Whether the membrane module was fitted for it is [answer].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-096", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "A multi-day run with the photometer logging continuously, to establish that the loop is stable over the timescale a field deployment needs.", "title": "Nobody outside the team has touched it", "body": "Five of these items are experiments and artifacts and none of them is a person. Ballot question two asks about user testing, and no one outside the hardware sub-team is named anywhere on this page. The cheapest answer is one person building from 6.2 and writing down where they got stuck, which tests the documentation question in the same afternoon. UBC ran five sessions on a bioreactor of its own and named the testers and their expertise.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2024-UG-ubc-03", "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-05"], "excerpt_note": "UBC writes user testing as an event with a place, a date, a named contact outside the team and a count of sessions.", "ballot": "Did the team conduct user testing and learn from user feedback?"}, {"id": "br-097", "type": "praise", "anchor": "A costed BOM against a named commercial perfusion system.", "title": "A deployability list, not a wish list", "body": "Five items, each an experiment or an artifact with a purpose stated in the same line, and this last one names the comparison that would make the cost argument land. A judge reads this as a plan instead of an apology.", "excerpts": ["hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-04"], "excerpt_note": "Humboldt gives the reason for publishing a price before the price, because other iGEM teams are short of money. Your last bullet is that reason turned into a task."}, {"id": "br-098", "type": "cut", "anchor": "With a constrained timescale, the team could not complete everything it had in mind.", "title": "Start with the two goals", "body": "The first clause apologises and the second names the two goals. A judge only needs the second.", "after": "Two goals are still open: the three-chamber bioreactor, and a quick-slot refill for B. subtilis and medium.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-099", "type": "cut", "anchor": "The three‑chamber concept arose while we were researching which protectants our B. subtilis should produce and secrete.", "title": "The protectant search is wet lab", "body": "Two paragraphs here explain how three protectants were chosen and which stresses they target. That argument belongs to the wet lab and to the Protein Design page. The hardware consequence is one sentence.", "after": "Three protectants mean three isolated cultures, each with its own light. That is the three-chamber concept: three compartments, switched green or red to set which protectant is made and secreted.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-100", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "ACC deaminase, LEA peptide and BoPep4", "title": "Two protectant names are wrong", "body": "Our Protein Design pages write BoPEP4 throughout, and the LEA molecule is LEA14, which those pages describe as \"the ordered/globular protein LEA14\" and deliberately distinguish from the disordered peptides in the family. \"LEA peptide\" and \"BoPep4\" appear only here. Use the names the wet lab uses.", "after": "ACC deaminase, LEA14 and BoPEP4", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-101", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The lid lifts, the front window clears, and the camera moves down the three chambers in turn.", "title": "A camera and a channel appear once", "body": "This caption introduces a camera that appears nowhere else on the page, and calls the package \"Three-channel\" where the heading above says three-chamber and the body says \"single-channel prototype\". Pick chamber or channel and use it everywhere, and either explain the camera or leave it out of the caption.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "br-102", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Nothing here has been built — it is the packaged arrangement the single‑channel prototype is a step toward", "title": "A CAD figure labelled unbuilt", "body": "A rendered concept is the easiest place on a hardware page to mislead a judge, and this caption says in its first clause that nothing in the image exists. It then says what the figure is for, which is the volume the machine would have to fit into.", "excerpts": ["hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06"], "excerpt_note": "TUDarmstadt reports a component unfinished in the same paragraph as its specification. Yours labels a whole figure as unbuilt."}], "excerpts": {"hardware-2023-OG-evry-04": {"id": "hardware-2023-OG-evry-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Evry-Paris-Saclay", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/evry-paris-saclay/hardware", "section": "Lux Sensor", "function": "section-intro", "text": "Given the need to calibrate our light illuminator and to enhance the precision of our protocols, combined with a tight budget, we decided to create our own light intensity (lux) detector (Figure 5). This decision was particularly valuable for our optogenetic project, as acquiring a professional probe spectrometer wasn't feasible within our experimental timeline.", "why_it_works": "A second device is justified in one sentence by the first device's need: the illuminator had to be calibrated and a commercial probe was out of budget. The constraint is stated plainly instead of being hidden.", "teaches": ["structure", "clarity", "honesty"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2023-OG-evry-05": {"id": "hardware-2023-OG-evry-05", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Evry-Paris-Saclay", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/evry-paris-saclay/hardware", "section": "Digital microfluidics lab automation platform", "function": "method", "text": "However, as we delved deeper into the project, we realized that automation could be beneficial beyond just the screening phase. It could streamline various other protocols integral to our work. We initially considered high-throughput solutions, particularly robotic liquid handlers. But given our budget, performance needs, and tight timelines, we decided against it.", "why_it_works": "The rejected alternative is named, costed in three words (budget, performance, timelines) and dropped, then the next alternative is narrowed the same way. A chain of two rejections is what makes the final choice look reasoned.", "teaches": ["structure", "decisions", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-01": {"id": "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/aachen/hardware", "section": "Affordable Personalized Cancer Care", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Our project directly addresses these challenges by developing a microfluidic chip system - CRC-on-a-Chip - where human HT-29 colorectal cancer cells are cultured, along with the MAIK measurement system. Designed as a rapid test, this concept offers a low-cost and accessible alternative for quickly assessing therapy effectiveness on patient-specific tumor samples. By providing a simple and affordable solution, it has the potential to bring personalized medicine to resource-limited settings, where traditional diagnostics are often out of reach.", "why_it_works": "The opening names the need, the device, and who it is for in three sentences, and the words that matter are low-cost, rapid and resource-limited settings. The reader knows what problem the hardware solves before any component appears.", "teaches": ["clarity", "structure", "audience"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-02": {"id": "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-02", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/aachen/hardware", "section": "What is MAIK?", "function": "reuse", "text": "MAIK is a flexible, easily customizable measurement system designed for colorimetric applications. It consists of a tailor-made casing that securely houses sensors, LEDs, and your sample to ensure accurate and reproducible measurements. Whether you’re working in synthetic biology, chemistry, or physics, MAIK provides you with the tools to make your experiments more efficient and precise.", "why_it_works": "The device is described to a stranger who might build it, in the second person: your sample, your experiments. Naming three fields it suits is a concrete claim of usefulness beyond the project.", "teaches": ["reuse", "audience", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-03": {"id": "hardware-2024-OG-aachen-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/aachen/hardware", "section": "Results", "function": "limitation", "text": "In addition to Ponceau S, we also measured Resazurin. While the results were not as optimal as those obtained with Ponceau S, the calibration curve for Resazurin still provided usable data. Although the sensitivity and accuracy of the Resazurin measurements were slightly lower, our system remains effective for applications involving this dye. Importantly, we were still able to collect and analyze samples with Resazurin, yielding results that, while not as precise, were sufficient for interpretation and further experimentation.", "why_it_works": "A second, worse result is reported rather than dropped, and the degradation is graded: less optimal, slightly lower sensitivity, still usable. The reader is told how much worse, and what it is still good for.", "teaches": ["honesty", "evidence", "uncertainty"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-01": {"id": "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "UBC-Vancouver", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ubc-vancouver/hardware/bioreactor/", "section": "Testing Mk. 1", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "To ensure proper bubbling in our bioreactor, we adjusted the current and voltage to the DC motor using a power supply until it produced the desired bubbling rate (2 / sec). However, we realized that these metrics changed depending on the depth of the tubing. We decided to set a standard metric at 100 mL below surface level and found that 9 V and 0.9 A produced the desired bubbling rate. With this test, we were able to establish that a 9V battery would be a sufficient power source for mark 1.", "why_it_works": "A target is set (two bubbles per second), the measurement is found to depend on tubing depth, so a standard depth is fixed and only then is the working voltage and current reported. Defining the condition is what makes the number repeatable.", "teaches": ["reproducibility", "evidence", "measurement"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-02": {"id": "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-02", "prize": "hardware", "team": "UBC-Vancouver", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ubc-vancouver/hardware/bioreactor/", "section": "Lessons Learned from Mk.1 (Internally)", "function": "limitation", "text": "Considering the time we spent using a Dremel tool to sand parts down, it would probably be worth our while in the future to add tolerances and extra space to our parts due to 3D printer error that can cause abrasiveness between moving parts or prints that have fits to them.", "why_it_works": "A build annoyance is turned into a transferable design rule: add tolerance for printer error on parts that have to fit or move. The lesson is stated as what they would do next time, not as an excuse.", "teaches": ["honesty", "reuse", "decisions"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-03": {"id": "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "UBC-Vancouver", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ubc-vancouver/hardware/bioreactor/", "section": "Learning through iHP Integration & User Feedback", "function": "audience-framing", "text": "We attempted to gain user feedback on Mk.1 from local collaborators that were external to our team. To begin, we took our bioreactor to the Biomedical Research Facility at UBC with our iHP contact Maggie Chopra, we identified four key areas of progression that future work can target:", "why_it_works": "User testing is written as an event with a place, a named contact outside the team, and a count of what came back. The four areas are then listed as problems with the device, not as compliments.", "teaches": ["user testing", "honesty", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-04": {"id": "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "UBC-Vancouver", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ubc-vancouver/hardware/bioreactor/", "section": "Pressure Relief Valve", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "Taking from experiences on Mark 1, internal pressure relief is an important part of being able to inflow and outflow reagents and substances. This provides a passive filtered-air inflow and outflow which could help heat and cool the bioreactor depending on its location.", "why_it_works": "A feature is introduced by the earlier prototype that forced it. One clause ties the pressure relief valve back to a problem the reader has already been told about, which is what makes an iteration read as learning.", "teaches": ["user testing", "structure", "decisions"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-05": {"id": "hardware-2024-UG-ubc-05", "prize": "hardware", "team": "UBC-Vancouver", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/ubc-vancouver/hardware/bioreactor/", "section": "Learning through iHP Integration & User Feedback", "function": "audience-framing", "text": "Once again, we incorporated iHP feedback from two external stakeholders, Ahmed Kabil & Taehyeon Kim from the Kelly McNagny Lab. These are both senior PhD Candidates exploring Immunology & Stem Cell Biology at UBC. They provided valuable insights into the potential applications and limitations of our bioreactors in their immunological research, and made design suggestions that would allow the bioreactors to potentially be used for Mammalian cells.", "why_it_works": "The testers are named, their expertise is stated, and what their expertise bought is specific: suggestions for extending the reactor to mammalian cells. Credentials are given because they explain why this feedback was worth acting on.", "teaches": ["user testing", "audience", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-01": {"id": "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TUDarmstadt", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/tudarmstadt/hardware", "section": "Motivation", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Microgravity significantly alters cellular growth, mobility, gene expression and biofilm formation [1, 2]. As parabolic flights are too short for long biological experiments and launches are expensive and limited, random positioning machines (RPMs) are used to simulate microgravity for an extended time in the lab. However, as commercial RPMs cost around 8.000$-50.000$ [3] they are inaccessible to many student labs and iGEM teams. Luckily, there are existing DIY solutions [3]; We discovered two GitLab projects describing DIY RPMs with a protocol and STL-data for printing (by Wahab Kawafi and Core Electronics). However, neither was validated to prove real microgravity simulation, and most published RPMs are either too expensive or described in overly complex, inaccessible papers [4].", "why_it_works": "The need is built in four moves: why microgravity matters, why existing methods fail, what commercial kit costs, and what the two existing DIY designs still lack. The gap the project fills is the last clause, that neither was validated.", "teaches": ["clarity", "structure", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06": {"id": "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-06", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TUDarmstadt", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/tudarmstadt/hardware", "section": "OD Measurement", "function": "limitation", "text": "To accurately monitor the cell density of Bacillus subtilis culture, we developed a DIY optical density (OD) sensor. It uses an orange LED (emission spectrum 590–625 nm) and a photodiode to measure the turbidity of the liquid culture. We successfully built a working circuit for the optical sensor, but could not test or integrate it into the bioreactor within the project timeframe.", "why_it_works": "A component is described with its part-level detail and then reported unfinished in the same paragraph: built, but neither tested nor integrated in time. The admission sits with the thing it concerns rather than in a future-work list.", "teaches": ["honesty", "structure", "limitations"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-07": {"id": "hardware-2025-OG-tudarmstadt-07", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TUDarmstadt", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/tudarmstadt/hardware", "section": "Culture test:", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "In order to find out whether our DIY-bioreactor was able to support life we filled it with ~1.7 L LB Media and inoculated it with a 10 ml liquid culture of wild-type Bacillus subtilis 168. Growth progression was documented after 24 hours. Comparing the media after inoculation and 24 hours later, it is recognizable that it turned turbid after 24 hours caused by bacterial growth inside the bioreactor.", "why_it_works": "The functional test is a culture: volume, inoculum, strain, time and the observation that settles it. The instrument is shown to do the one thing it exists for, with enough numbers to run it again.", "teaches": ["evidence", "validation", "reproducibility"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-03": {"id": "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Cornell", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/cornell/hardware", "section": "Build for PRoSPER - and for your own ideas", "function": "method", "text": "The top section of the reactor is CNC-machined from aluminum alloy and equipped with four stainless steel hose barbs. The combination of stainless steel, aluminum alloy, and internal silicone tubing makes the entire assembly autoclavable. Based on this, we designed a dedicated autoclave configuration, which allows the sterilized components to be assembled later with non-autoclavable parts. This ensure a rigorous and contamination-free sterile workflow.", "why_it_works": "Material choices are listed and then justified by one consequence: the assembly can be autoclaved. The sterile workflow that follows is a design decision about where the instrument stops and the biology begins.", "teaches": ["decisions", "reproducibility", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-04": {"id": "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Cornell", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/cornell/hardware", "section": "Build for PRoSPER - and for your own ideas", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Each COB LED module is equipped with a 12V 16W COB LED panel capable of delivering up to 1000 lumens of brightness. At the working distance within ARC, a single panel can provide up to 37 klux of brightness, which is more than sufficient to support Synechococcus which requires 3000 lux. In the final tests, only 20% brightness was needed to achieve optimal illumination with 2 modules.", "why_it_works": "Three numbers in order: what the part can deliver, what the organism needs, and what was actually used. Stating the requirement next to the capability turns a specification into evidence of fitness.", "teaches": ["evidence", "measurement", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-06": {"id": "hardware-2025-UG-cornell-06", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Cornell", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/cornell/hardware", "section": "Build for PRoSPER - and for your own ideas", "function": "reuse", "text": "Meanwhile, we also prepared a general operating manual containing detailed assembly guidelines and operation guidelines. It contains detailed descriptions of the precautions in operation, the assembly of autoclave configuration, and the specific structural differences among different variants.", "why_it_works": "The deliverable another team needs is named and its contents are itemised: assembly, operation, precautions, and the differences between variants. One sentence tells the reader the manual exists and what is in it.", "teaches": ["reuse", "reproducibility", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-01": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2015, "track": null, "url": "https://2015.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Lab/Bioreactor/Characterization_of_Bioreactor", "section": "Final Experiment", "function": "method", "text": "The experiment for characterization of the chemostat bioreactor consisted of a batch mode followed by the continuous mode with 3 different dilution rates. We used BL21 Gold (DE3) pSB1KRDP strain in minimal carbon medium M9. The experiment was designed to keep the dilution rates constant for 3 reactor volumes, so as to obtain constant optical density, an indication of the biomass, within human sampling error, to achieve proof of concept.", "why_it_works": "States the run structure, the strain and the medium, then gives the success criterion before any result: hold each dilution rate for three reactor volumes and see whether OD stays constant within sampling error. The reader knows what would have counted as failure.", "teaches": ["method", "structure", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-02": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-02", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2015, "track": null, "url": "https://2015.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Lab/Bioreactor/Characterization_of_Bioreactor", "section": "Final Experiment", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "The continuous mode of operation was initiated with a dilution rate of 0.1 h-1 at hour 5. Constant OD was obtained for 9 hours. However, the feed pump was observed to be pumping more medium than the harvest removed by the harvest pump. Hence at our 30, the Master Control Program was modified to change the speed of the harvest pump accordingly. The harvest pump rate was changed to pump 30 % faster than the feed pump. The change made is reflected in reduction of OD from an average value of 0.78 to 0.76.", "why_it_works": "A control fault found mid-run is reported in sequence: what was set, how long it held, what went wrong with the pump balance, what was changed and by how much, and the measured effect on OD. Nothing is hidden and the cost of the fix is given as a number.", "teaches": ["honesty", "evidence", "method"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-03": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2015, "track": null, "url": "https://2015.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Lab/Bioreactor/Hardware", "section": "Measurement Principle", "function": "limitation", "text": "To test if our continuous OD device can also be used to monitor batch fermentations, we set up a simple batch culture. We used a preculture of Eschererichia coli BL21 Gold (DE3) with plasmid pSB1KRDP in M9 medium with 40 mM glucose that was grown in a stirred flask. A pump was used to cycle the culture from the vessel through the continuous OD sensor and back into the vessel. This setup was far from being perfect from a bioprocess perspective, but the goal of this experiment was testing the OD sensor and not the generation of biologically relevant samples.", "why_it_works": "Gives the full protocol for a side experiment, then says plainly what the experiment was not for. Fencing off the claim stops a reader mistaking a sensor test for a bioprocess result.", "teaches": ["honesty", "limitation", "method"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-04": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2015-aachen-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2015, "track": null, "url": "https://2015.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Notebook/Construction_Manuals", "section": "Cost Estimation to Create your Own Bioreactor", "function": "section-intro", "text": "The following table gives you an overview of the costs for a complete system consisting of three bioreactors with three pumps and a biomass sensor each.", "why_it_works": "One sentence fixes the scope of the cost table before the numbers: three reactors, three pumps each, one biomass sensor each. A total is only checkable if the thing being totalled is named.", "teaches": ["clarity", "reproducibility", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-01": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-01", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Humboldt Berlin", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:Humboldt_Berlin/Hardware", "section": "Introduction", "function": "page-opening", "text": "The openPBR is a low-cost, easy to assemble and reliable option to cultivate phototrophic organisms. We have developed it as an open-source platform to operate as you need it. It comes in several setups, from nine cultivation vessels to three flat panels with a minimum width of 15 mm and up to a volume of 190 ml. It provides users with the ability to measure optical density at wavelengths from 400 nm to 800 nm, online and provides a .csv file output. All parts are openly available and even gas supply is exactly adjustable through a rotameter, depending on the needs of your organism and setup. We want to offer scientists a simple and versatile cultivation platform.", "why_it_works": "The opening doubles as a specification sheet: configurations, vessel widths, volumes, wavelength range, output format. A reader can decide from one paragraph whether the instrument fits their organism.", "teaches": ["clarity", "structure", "reproducibility"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-03": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Humboldt Berlin", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:Humboldt_Berlin/Hardware", "section": "Calibration", "function": "limitation", "text": "From this we can conclude that, while the openPBR measurements between OD = 0 and OD = 1 are quite reliable, further calibrating points are needed in order to improve the range beyond OD = 1.", "why_it_works": "Converts the validation plot into a working range and says what would extend it. The limit is a number a user can respect rather than a general caution.", "teaches": ["limitation", "honesty", "uncertainty"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-04": {"id": "hwp-bioreactor-2019-humboldt-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Humboldt Berlin", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:Humboldt_Berlin/Hardware", "section": "Pricing", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Since we are a non-profit team of our own, we know that financing is one of the limiting and most problematic challenges of this contest. So it was important to us, that every iGEM team who wants to continue our project ,or any new project using a phototrophic organism, has a low-price but efficient way to fulfill their great ideas. For only 588 € it is possible to rebuild our bioreactor that offers many opportunities to design a set-up tailored to the needs of innovative projects.", "why_it_works": "Gives the reason for publishing a price before giving the price: other iGEM teams are short of money. The total is a single figure a team can hold against its own budget.", "teaches": ["reuse", "audience-framing", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-05": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-05", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2014, "track": null, "url": "https://2014.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Notebook/Engineering/ODF", "section": "Experiments", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "It is interesting to observer that function $g$, mapping the true OD of our device to the true OD of the photospectrometer, are close together for both P. putida and S. cerivisae, as seen by the regression coefficient. In fact, 3.416 and 3.461 are such close together, that the minor deviation could be just measuring inaccuracy. Therefore, we fix the regression coefficient for converting true OD of our device to true OD of the photospectrometer to an average of 3.432 .", "why_it_works": "Two independently measured calibration coefficients are reported, then judged: the gap between them is small enough to be measurement error, so they are averaged into one fixed constant. The reasoning for collapsing them is shown rather than assumed.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "honesty"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2014-aachen-06", "prize": "hardware", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2014, "track": null, "url": "https://2014.igem.org/Team:Aachen/Notebook/Engineering/ODF", "section": "Construction Steps", "function": "method", "text": "Next we want to assemble the cuvette holders. On the side with the square hole attach the light-to-frequency sensor with glue. For the OD case place the orange LED opposite, or for fluorescence, the LED in the hole in the bottom. Make sure to close any remaining open hole! Please attach a piece of filter foil (approx. $7 \\times 7 mm^2$) from the inside in front of the light to frequency converter. Forceps is highly recommended.", "why_it_works": "An assembly step written for someone holding the parts: what to glue where, what changes between the OD and the fluorescence build, a dimension for the filter foil, a warning not to leave a hole open, and the tool that makes it possible.", "teaches": ["reproducibility", "method", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-02": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-02", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TU Darmstadt", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:TU_Darmstadt/Tech", "section": "Do it yourself", "function": "method", "text": "To determine a regression, the values of the SensorBrick are plotted against the OD of the photometer. The Pearson coefficient of 0.98 proofs the correlation of both measured optical densities is suitable. The function of the regression in Fig. 3, can now be used to calculate the exact OD600 from the data measured with our SensorBrick.", "why_it_works": "States how the two instruments were read against each other, gives the correlation coefficient, and then says what the fit is for: converting the sensor's raw value into an OD. Calibration is presented as a transform with a provenance.", "teaches": ["method", "evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-03": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-03", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TU Darmstadt", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:TU_Darmstadt/Tech", "section": "Do it yourself", "function": "validation", "text": "Now that the SensorBrick is calibrated, the value of the SensorBrick can be inserted into the given function, so that an absolute value of the OD can be obtained. To verify our calibration we ran another two experiments and compared the data to the photometer. We grew E. coli (Fig. 5) and then transformed E. coli with the dual expression vector (Fig. 6).", "why_it_works": "Separates calibration from validation in one sentence: having fitted the transform, they ran two further experiments and compared again. Naming the held-out runs is what makes the earlier coefficient mean something.", "teaches": ["validation", "method", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-04": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-04", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TU Darmstadt", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:TU_Darmstadt/Tech", "section": "Do it yourself", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "In the following we will compare the calibrated OD600 values of the SensorBrick with the measured data from the photometer. In Fig. 5 we see that the OD600 of the SensorBrick is lower. This might be due to us having used the dual expression vector pTeTW3con2, expressing superfolder green fluorescent protein (sfGFP), for calibrating the SensorBrick. As a result, the expressed fluorescence increased the intensity of light in the range of 600 nm, so the calculated values of OD600 appear lower than they actually are. The deviation of the calculated values to the photometer can be explained by the increasing amount of fluorescence during the time of expression.", "why_it_works": "Reports that their own instrument reads low, then explains the mechanism: they calibrated on a strain expressing sfGFP, whose fluorescence added light at the measured wavelength. A disagreement is turned into a diagnosis instead of being smoothed over.", "teaches": ["honesty", "uncertainty", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}, "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-05": {"id": "hwp-photometer-2019-tu-darmstadt-05", "prize": "hardware", "team": "TU Darmstadt", "year": 2019, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2019.igem.org/Team:TU_Darmstadt/Tech", "section": "Do it yourself", "function": "limitation", "text": "Fig. 6 shows that a calibrated SenorBrick determines approximate values above an OD600 of 0.3. We learned that it is necessary to calibrate the SensorBrick with the used bacteria strain individually. It can not be universally calibrated, especially when fluorescent proteins are present. In order to achieve this, the sensor would need to persists of a monochromator. Therefore, the OD600 measurement by the SensorBrick can only be seen as qualitative values. This still suits the purpose of determining the exact moment for the web app to initiate the induction.", "why_it_works": "Gives the threshold above which the reading is usable, draws the general rule (it cannot be calibrated once for every strain), names the part that would be needed to fix it, downgrades its own claim to qualitative, then says why the downgraded claim still does the job.", "teaches": ["limitation", "honesty", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Best Hardware"}}};
